        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a9c9493e-3269-57ca-8887-6f451b5a6452",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jack Young, Arthur Payne\n- Events: 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship, London Riders' Championship\n- Topics: speedway, South Australian Championship\n\nFacts:\n- In 1952, Jack Young became the first dual winner of the Speedway World Championship and the first rider to win the title in two consecutive years by winning his second straight championship with 14 points.\n- In 1952, Arthur Payne finished fifth in the Speedway World Championship final.\n- Arthur Payne was born on 28 August 1923 in Sydney, New South Wales.\n- Arthur Payne was an Australian international speedway rider.\n- Jack Young won the London Riders' Championship in 1953 and again in 1954.\n- Jack Young was a nine-time South Australian Champion between 1948 and 1964.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "82a0374e-1305-571b-8c06-a96205737cc6",
        "text": "Page: 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship. Sentence: Australian rider Jack Young became the first ever dual winner (and the first to win two in a row) when he won his second straight World Championship after scoring 14 points.\nPage: Jack Young (speedway rider). Sentence: He also won the London Riders' Championship 1953 and 1954 and was a nine time South Australian Champion between 1948 and 1964.\nPage: Arthur Payne (speedway rider). Sentence: Arthur Payne (born 28 August 1923 in Sydney, New South Wales) was an Australian international speedway rider who finished fifth in the 1952 Speedway World Championship final.",
